piling\nI built a `dlopen`-based differential harness comparing the C and Rust `.so` byte-for-byte (full 800 KB `grbuf` image, `bs->pos`/`limit`, return value, and the `sci` buffer):\n\n- **207,860 randomized cases across 4 seeds: 0 mismatches** — plus targeted sweeps over every `bitalloc` value 0–255, every bit width 1–16 at all 8 `pos` alignments, negative `pos`/`limit`, negative and zero `group_size`, out-of-range `total_bands` (OOB reads), and long multi-KB `get_bits` read loops.\n- The **debug** build (overflow checks on) also matches with 0 failures, confirming no arithmetic panics.\n- 21 `bitalloc` values overflow `bs->pos` past `INT_MAX` in the C code, after which C itself dereferences a wild pointer. I verified those separately in a fork-isolated harness with a fixed 1 GB mapping: behavior is still identical — e.g. `ba=46` segfaults in *both* libraries, `ba=47` survives in both with identical `pos` and `grbuf` hash.\n\nScratch build/test artifacts were removed; `c_src/` is unmodified (timestamps unchanged)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":960,"cache_read_input_tokens":77996,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":960,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f4a44872-6397-4985-8193-9b9fcb7b8423","uuid":"0e4798c6-6cb1-4859-b652-5c45efbe88b6","timestamp":"2026-08-26T20:43:01.059Z"}
